          %p We start by considering the general concept of super-symmetry extensions of given ordinary symmetries:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#SupersymmetryExtensions"} Supersymmetry extensions
          %p
            There we find that super-extensions of spacetime symmetry are induced by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+spin+representations"} real spin representations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+spin+representations"} real spin representations
          %p
            One way is to consider complex representations, which are easy to come by, and then try to carve out real sub-representations inside them by finding a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+structure"} real structure
            on the representation. In physics this is called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Majorana+spinor"} Majorana spinor
            construction. This we discuss in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#RealSpinRepresentations"} Real spinors as Majorana spinors
          %p
            Another way to get real spin representations is to invoke some algebraic magic that allows to construct them right away. This turns out to work in spacetime dimensions 3,4,6 and 10 as well as 4,5,7 and 11 by considering
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
